{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1478 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f7b6c10c0aac6522e6c4ca15866e4413295e807d5977a13c89187f9ebefcc7ee" :edb/db-before-t 1477 :edb/db-after-t 1478 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534790 50 #inst "2026-10-12T13:59:11.970Z" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186123920 1078 #edb/ref 17592186123921 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186124452 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436125 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186124452 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436126 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186124452 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436129 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186124453 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436123 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186124453 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436128 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186124457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436127 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186124558 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436124 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186124558 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436130 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186124724 1050 900.0 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186124953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122318 13194139534790 17592186045417 false] [17592186125220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186125220 1071 "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}.$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186125220 1071 "Find $lim_(x → (-1, 2, 2))\\frac{z^{2} - y^{2}}{z^{3} - y^{2}z}$." 13194139534790 17592186045417 false] [17592186125220 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}=\\frac{{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{{2}^{3}-{2}^{2}\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{z({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{1}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{1}{z} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186125221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123926 13194139534790 17592186045417 false] [17592186125731 1064 "We can extend our knowledge of limits to multivariable functions. We'll first consider the two-variable case.\n\nWe denote $\\mathbf{x}=(x,y)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0 = (a,b)$ as a particular, fixed vector. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined in some neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$\n\nThen, the notation\n\n$$\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f (x,y) = L$$\n\nmeans that the value of $f$ can be made as close as we want to $L,$ provided that $\\mathbf x$ is sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- This definition of a limit also extends to functions of more than two variables in the natural way.\n- All of the rules set out by the algebra of limits are valid for functions of several variables.\n\nWe say that $f(x,y)$ is **continuous** at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (a,b)} f (x,y) = f(a,b).$$\n\nSo, if a function is continuous at $(a,b),$ then the limit can be evaluated by simply plugging $(x,y) = (a,b)$ into the function.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following limit: $$\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}$$\n\nThis function is continuous for all $(x,y)$ with the exception of $(0,0)$ where the denominator vanishes. Since the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(x,y) = (2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 false] [17592186125731 1064 "We can extend our knowledge of limits to multivariable functions. We'll first consider the two-variable case. \n\nWe denote $\\mathbf{x}=(x,y)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0 = (a,b)$ as a particular, fixed vector. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined in some neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ \n\nThen, the notation\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f (x,y) = L\n$$\n\nmeans that the value of $f$ can be made as close as we want to $L,$ provided that $\\mathbf x$ is sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- This definition of a limit also extends to functions of more than two variables in the natural way.\n\n- All of the rules set out by the algebra of limits are valid for functions of several variables.\n\nWe say that $f(x,y)$ is **continuous** at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (a,b)} f (x,y) = f(a,b).\n$$\n\nSo, if a function is continuous at $(a,b),$ then the limit can be evaluated by simply plugging $(x,y) = (a,b)$ into the function.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous for all $(x,y)$ with the exception of $(0,0)$ where the denominator vanishes. Since the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(x,y) = (2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186335802 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $0$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 false] [17592186335802 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\nI. The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n\nII. The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$\n\nIII. The limit exists and is equal to $0$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186380062 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $1$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 false] [17592186380062 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nI. The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n\nII. The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n\nIII. The limit exists and is equal to $1$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436075 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436075 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436075 1083 #uuid "034d1791-5b87-5fc0-88f4-c35a94cf6f24" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436076 1080 "6" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436076 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436076 1083 #uuid "08e5ed22-82cd-5dd4-ae2b-e1265c44598a" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436077 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436077 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436077 1083 #uuid "0924a936-e922-5c55-aa02-d684eeb3f2b8" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436078 1080 "1" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436078 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436078 1083 #uuid "13a55cd8-59d4-51e6-bfc3-6f7e89d540fc" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [1