{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}x+y & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436120 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436124 1069 "q-74962" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436124 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436124 1072 #uuid "6e200150-12cc-5141-a4cc-a34826db19ce" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436124 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{3+(-3)+4}-2}=\\frac{6-6}{\\sqrt{4}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y+4-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{z(x+y)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2) & =2(\\sqrt{3-3+4}+2) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{4}+2) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $8.$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436116 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436125 1069 "q-129323" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436125 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436125 1072 #uuid "7e76204f-7492-5638-91de-b17ed7af74ee" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436125 1074 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y} & =\\frac{2(2\\cdot 1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436119 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436126 1069 "q-74908" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436126 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}.$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436126 1072 #uuid "88f91bc6-5d92-5d89-9639-1db1ee91906d" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436126 1074 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the expression under the radical   is negative. However, for the point $(-2,-2)$ the product $x\\cdot y$ is not negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,-2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot (-2)}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436117 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436127 1069 "q-129402" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436127 1071 "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436127 1072 #uuid "9df4444a-990b-5f68-bec9-ae4f2c45450b" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436127 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{({0}^{2})(0)}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}(0)}{{x}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436118 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436128 1069 "q-129336" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436128 1071 "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y}.$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436128 1072 #uuid "a678f4db-f59f-58b6-9d8f-ba8fadd30c17" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436128 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}+2(1)^{2}(-1)+1(-1)^{2}}{1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2})}{x+y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+y)(x+y)}{x+y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+y)\\cancel{(x+y)}}{\\cancel{x+y}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}x(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}x(x+y) & =1(1-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436122 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436129 1069 "q-74911" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436129 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}=$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436129 1072 #uuid "c11b4e02-4fa9-528c-867b-1ff09ac00c10" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436129 1074 "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(4,-4,-2)$ does not cause the  denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(4,-4,-2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z} & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{4-(-4)}}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{8}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436121 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436130 1069 "q-74959" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436130 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436130 1072 #uuid "cdbbe1e3-c4b9-56df-b019-b8be69ef537e" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true] [17592186436130 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{6+3}-3}{6+3-{3}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{6+y}-x)(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{6+y})^{2}-{x}^{2}}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{6+y-{x}^{2}}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+y}+x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+y}+x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+3}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{6}.$" 13194139534790 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-034d1791-5b87-5fc0-88f4-c35a94cf6f24" 17592186436075 "answer-08e5ed22-82cd-5dd4-ae2b-e1265c44598a" 17592186436076 "answer-0924a936-e922-5c55-aa02-d684eeb3f2b8" 17592186436077 "answer-13a55cd8-59d4-51e6-bfc3-6f7e89d540fc" 17592186436078 "answer-15697aa4-8790-5d84-b361-1fcd5c853cfd" 17592186436079 "answer-1e050b6b-4c71-5b1d-95f2-c237914af9e0" 17592186436080 "answer-23db4813-aed1-5501-99cf-12aa83f3383d" 17592186436081 "answer-289a71e6-833a-5782-99bf-9eaaf974a1d9" 1759